From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Johannes Stein <johannesstein@freeze-dev.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 09:43:22 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 64/77] function references: call a converted method on the
 instance taken at the conversion (#42006)

Converting a method of an object expression to a function reference
("P := O.Method" in mode delphi) moved the expression O into the Invoke
method of the capturer without capturing anything it uses
(capturer_add_procvar_or_proc took tloadnode(n).left into the call node
and cleared it before the capture scans ran over n, and the scan only
looked at symbols of nested routines). The Invoke method then read a
local variable or parameter of the converting routine from its own
frame, a field of Self through the capturer, or a global variable that
the converting routine kept in a register: the method ran with a
garbage Self or the capturer as Self (#42006: runtime error 216 on
x86_64, wrong results on every target), and wasm32 with register
variables stopped with internal error 2021092006 (tests tfuncref9 and
tfuncref45 at -O2, whose methods do not use Self).

- A method of a class, interface or class reference expression other
  than Self: evaluate the expression once, at the conversion, and keep
  it in a field of the capturer, as is already done for procvars; the
  Invoke method calls the method on that field (Delphi semantics: a
  later change of the variable does not change the target).
- Any other instance (a record, an object, Self): the expression stays
  in the Invoke method, so capture the local variables and parameters
  it uses (as an anonymous function would; a var parameter cannot be
  captured and is now reported instead of being read from a wrong
  frame) and keep the global variables it uses in memory.
- Conversions of a procvar or of a method of an instance get their own
  Invoke method and field per conversion site: before, two conversions
  of the same procvar or method in one routine shared the first one's
  field or instance ("P := V; V := W; Q := V" made Q call the old V).

Tests: tfuncref57 (parameter, var parameter, local, field of another
class, function result, typecast, class methods, interface, record,
nested routine, global; value taken at the conversion), tfuncref58
(procvar converted twice), tfuncref59 (interface lifetime, anonymous
function, generic routine, record field of Self, two instances in one
routine).
